  "step_2": "The individual reports: 'Ich habe mir die Beträge angeschaut und die Lotterie gewählt mit der ich den höheren Betrag gewinnen könnte' (I looked at the amounts and chose the lottery with which I could win the higher amount). This indicates that the individual focused only on the maximum possible monetary outcome (4000 vs 3000) and used this as the basis for the decision. The individual did not mention probabilities, distributions, or any comparison of likelihoods. The justification is purely outcome-based, centered on the potential to win the highest amount, regardless of its probability. There is no reference to the shape of the distribution, concentration of probability, or skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness, i.e., the concentration of probability on favorable outcomes. This involves comparing both the values and their probabilities to determine which lottery has more probability mass on its favorable outcome. The individual, however, only considered the magnitude of the highest possible outcome (4000 vs 3000) and ignored probabilities entirely. Since the decision reason depends on probability distribution and the individual did not consider probabilities or distributional shape, the relevant information (skewness) was not considered. Furthermore, the justification (choosing based on highest possible gain) differs fundamentally from the rule of the decision reason (choosing based on greater probability mass on favorable outcomes). Therefore, there is no alignment between the information used or the justification provided and the decision reason. The individual’s reasoning contradicts the decision reason, which would favor B (more left-skewed), while the individual’s logic leads to A (higher maximum).",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
