  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing and comparing the skewness of the lotteries—specifically, how concentrated the probability is on favorable outcomes. This is conceptually similar to what the individual considered, as 'a win being more likely' corresponds to the probability of a favorable (non-zero) outcome. Both Lottery A and B have only one non-zero outcome, so the 'likelihood of a win' directly reflects the probability of the favorable outcome. In this case, Lottery B has a higher probability of a win (90% vs 45%), so both the reason and the individual would focus on this and prefer B. Although the term 'skewness' is not used, the individual’s focus on the higher probability of winning aligns with the core idea of left-skewness in binary-outcome lotteries. Therefore, the information considered and the justification match the decision reason closely, even if not articulated in statistical terms.",
