  "step_2": "The individual reports having looked at all outcomes and probabilities and chose the lottery that promises the higher gain, because the probability of winning is similarly low in both lotteries. This indicates that the individual considered both the outcomes (amounts won) and the probabilities, but their justification centers on the higher potential gain (6000 vs 3000 Euros) as the decisive factor. They explicitly note the similarity in winning probabilities (0.1% vs 0.2%) and use this to downplay the difference in probabilities, elevating the importance of the outcome size. Thus, the individual’s reasoning is outcome-focused, prioritizing the magnitude of the prize under the condition that probabilities are both very low and similar. There is no mention of distribution of probabilities across outcomes, concentration of probability mass, or any concept resembling skewness.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating which lottery is more left-skewed, i.e., assigns more probability to favorable outcomes relative to unfavorable ones. As established, Lottery A has a higher probability of a favorable outcome (0.2% vs 0.1%), making it more left-skewed and thus preferred by the reason. However, the individual chose based on the higher potential gain (favoring Lottery B), not on the probability distribution favoring a positive outcome. The individual’s justification relies on outcome maximization under similar low probabilities, not on the skewness of the distribution. While both lotteries have low probabilities, the individual does not assess which has relatively better odds of winning, nor do they weigh the concentration of probability on gains. Instead, they use the small probability difference to justify focusing on the prize size. Therefore, the information used (outcome size, similarity in low probabilities) does not align with the skewness-relevant information (probability of favorable outcome), and the decision rule (choose higher prize when probabilities are similarly low) differs from the skewness rule (choose higher probability of gain). There is no evidence the individual considered skewness as a concept or applied its logic.",
  "final_assessment_score": 10
}

